<!-- ============================== DECISION MEMO ============================== -->
<div class="memo">
<div class="memo-head">
  <div class="kicker">Decision memo</div>
  <table class="mh"><tbody>
    <tr><th>To</th><td>Credit Risk Committee, Mainkai Direktbank AG (fictional)</td><th>Date</th><td>12 November 2026</td></tr>
    <tr><th>From</th><td>Fiona Edocket, Third-Party Model Review</td><th>Subject</th><td>Adoption of {{ v.score_name }} as the primary bureau score for consumer instalment loans</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
</div>

<div class="decision"><div class="kicker">Recommendation</div><div class="d">{{ outcome.decision }}</div>
<div class="r">Applied mechanically under outcome {{ outcome.rule }} of the scope: a core element, outcomes analysis, cannot be rated on public evidence. Findings: {{ counts['Critical']|default(0) }} Critical, {{ counts['High'] }} High, {{ counts['Medium'] }} Medium, {{ counts['Low'] }} Low.</div></div>

<div class="cols"><div>
<h3>Why not yet</h3>
<ol>
<li><b>No outcomes evidence for Mainkai's applicants.</b> The only public performance figure is a company-reported Gini of 0.6, with no sample, period or product. That figure covers the bureau's whole population and cannot be compared with a Gini on Mainkai's applicants. Mainkai's test is relative: on the same retro-scored applicants, the new score may not lose more than 3 Gini points against the current score, and must be calibrated by pricing band. Only a retrospective scoring of Mainkai's past applicants can show this (F02).</li>
<li><b>A legal question opens on 20 November 2026.</b> New §37a(2) BDSG bars address data from scores, and the Bundestag committee calls their use impermissible without exception. Criterion 4, age of current address, carries up to 94 points. {{ V }} has not publicly explained how it treats this criterion, and §37a conditions the lender's <i>use</i> as well as the score's creation (F01).</li>
<li><b>Thin-file applicants hit a ceiling.</b> In the calculator, a person one year into the German credit file cannot score above {{ summary.ceiling_at_12m_step }} even with a perfect record. That is Class 2. Reaching Class 1 takes between {{ F.class1_years_min }} and {{ F.class1_years_max }} years, depending on annual bands that are not published. No public data shows whether this matches observed risk for under-25s or recent arrivals (F03).</li>
</ol></div><div>
<h3>What is in good shape</h3>
<ul>
<li>All twelve criteria and their maximum points are public. The calculator reproduces every published value, and points move in the expected direction in every criterion.</li>
<li>Each bureau report shows points per criterion, so a person-level explanation of a decline is feasible today.</li>
<li>{{ V }} reports an external methodological review by Fraunhofer IAIS and an acceptance by the competent data protection authority. The reports themselves are not public (F05).</li>
</ul>
<h3>What would change the answer</h3>
<p>If {{ v.vendor }} supplies E01, E02, E03 and E07 by 31 January 2027, Model Validation can rate outcomes analysis. If no Critical finding emerges, the outcome rules then allow <b>approve with conditions</b>, with each High finding closed by a named owner before go-live (assumed 1 April 2027).</p>
</div></div>

<table class="memo-t"><thead><tr><th style="width:6%">ID</th><th style="width:52%">High finding: each closes through a named condition (section 4)</th><th style="width:26%">Owner</th><th style="width:16%">By</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for f in highs %}<tr><td><b>{{ f.id }}</b></td><td>{{ s(f.title) }}</td><td>{{ f.owner }}</td><td>{{ f.deadline.split(' (')[0] }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table>
<p class="small muted"><b>Residual risk if adopted with conditions:</b> concentration on one dominant bureau, a partial age proxy in time-based criteria, and legal uncertainty on §37a until a court or authority rules. These are risks to accept and monitor, not to close.</p>
</div>

<!-- ============================== 1. SCOPE AND METHOD ============================== -->
<h2 class="pb">1. What was reviewed and how</h2>
<p><b>Decision under review.</b> Mainkai Direktbank AG is a fictional Frankfurt direct bank supervised by BaFin, with about 450,000 retail customers. It wants to use {{ v.vendor }}'s new 12-criterion score as its primary bureau score for the Ratenkredit, an unsecured instalment loan of EUR 1,000 to 50,000 over 12 to 96 months. The score would set an auto-decline cut-off, one of five pricing bands and the maximum loan amount, across about 120,000 applications a year. Applicants in the middle band, about 20%, go to an underwriter with override authority. Mainkai's cut-offs are calibrated to the older bank-sector score, which {{ v.vendor }} is phasing out.</p>
<p><b>Roles.</b> {{ V }} is the provider and Mainkai the deployer under the AI Act, if the score is an AI system (section 6). Under GDPR, {{ v.vendor }} is an independent controller and Mainkai the controller for the credit decision. Applicants are data subjects and, under the AI Act, affected persons. The <i>role trap</i>: if Mainkai blended the score with its own data into a new model, it would become a provider. The scope uses the score as delivered, and cut-offs are policy rules.</p>
<p><b>Method.</b> The review has four strands. (1) A source register: {{ sources|length }} sources, each recorded as a text excerpt with a checksum (the two statutes, MaRisk and the act in BGBl. 2026 I Nr. 139, are also saved as original PDFs with SHA-256 hashes), tagged by tier, and linked to {{ n_claims }} claims, each labelled fact or assessment. (2) Lab A: a calculator built from the published points table and tested with unit tests (tests/test_calculator.py). (3) Lab B: an outcomes harness, run on {{ F.n_analysed }} Bondora loans as proxy evidence about a different lender and model. (4) A desk review against SR 26-2 and PRA SS1/23 elements A to L, with EBA GL/2020/06 section 4.3.4, MaRisk (Circular 06/2026), DORA, the AI Act as amended by Regulation (EU) 2026/1744, §37a BDSG, §505a BGB and GDPR Articles 15 and 22.</p>
<div class="box"><b>Evidence standard.</b> Each item is rated <i>publicly available and adequate</i>, <i>available but inadequate</i>, <i>not publicly available</i>, <i>not yet due</i> or <i>not applicable</i>. Absence from the public record is not a failing: {{ v.vendor }}'s evidence duties run to its lending customers under contract, so every gap is framed as something Mainkai should request. Calculator results describe the published table, not how {{ v.vendor }} scores real people.</div>
<p><b>Ratings.</b> <span class="pill r-Critical">Critical</span> means Mainkai could not lawfully use the score, or could not meet a deployer or §37a duty, and no fix is workable before go-live. <span class="pill r-High">High</span> means a material gap that a contract condition or pre-go-live evidence could close. <span class="pill r-Medium">Medium</span> means a gap to close within 6 months of go-live, and <span class="pill r-Low">Low</span> a good-practice improvement.</p>
<p class="small muted">Out of scope: independent validation, any audit or certification opinion, legal advice, Mainkai's affordability model, {{ v.vendor }}'s fraud and identity products, conformity assessment, IRB use and a full ISO/IEC 42001 assessment. The author is not independent of the work and had no access to {{ v.vendor }}'s data, code or contracts.</p>

<!-- ============================== 2. ARTEFACT: CALCULATOR ============================== -->
<h2>2. The artefact: a calculator built from the published points table</h2>
<p>{{ V }} publishes each criterion and the points it can earn. The calculator (<code>lab/calculator.py</code>) adds the twelve criteria together. <b>There is no base score.</b> The maxima sum to 999, and the floor of 100 comes from the payment criterion, which never falls below 100 while a score exists. An earlier draft of the plan added a 100-point base, which would have produced a range of 200 to 1,099. A unit test now prevents that error from returning.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th style="width:9%">Max</th><th style="width:12%">Sources</th><th style="width:15%">Evidence tier</th><th>Not published</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for c in criteria %}<tr><td>{{ c.label }}{% if c.legal %} <span class="tag">§37a flag</span>{% endif %}</td><td><b>{{ c.max }}</b></td><td class="small">{{ c.sources }}</td><td class="small">{{ c.tier }}</td><td class="small">{{ c.unpublished }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
<tr><td><b>Total</b></td><td><b>999</b></td><td colspan="3" class="small">Minimum 100. No score while a payment disruption is open. Reported classes: 776+, 709 to 775, 642 to 708, 100 to 641.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><b>Two readings of the gaps.</b> Some annual steps are not published. The calculator never invents them. The <i>step-hold</i> reading keeps the last published value until the next published point, which gives a lower bound. The <i>interpolated</i> reading draws a straight line between published points, which is illustrative only. Every result below says which reading it uses.</p>
<p><b>Tests (27, all passing).</b> Maximum 999 and minimum 100 under both readings. No base. Every time-based criterion is non-decreasing with age. Counts and remaining term are non-increasing. Payment recovery rises with time since settlement. Step-hold never exceeds the interpolated reading. Every published value is reproduced exactly, and an open disruption returns no score.</p>
<div class="fig"><img src="{{ img.weights }}"></div>
<p class="cap">Figure 1. Maximum points per criterion. Rust marks the four time-dependent criteria: {{ F.time_points }} of 999 points ({{ F.time_points_pct }}) can only be earned with time. Source: published points table; calculator.</p>

<h3>What the table does to thin-file applicants</h3>
<div class="fig"><img src="{{ img.ceiling }}"></div>
<p class="cap">Figure 2. Highest score the published table allows after a given time in the German credit file. The profile assumes perfect payment behaviour, no mortgage, and an address as old as the file. The solid line uses published values only; the dotted line interpolates. Under published values only, Class 1 is reached after {{ F.class1_years_max }} years. The bureau's unpublished values step once a year, so if they rise early Class 1 could be reached from {{ F.class1_years_min }} years (the calculator's upper reading); the published table cannot narrow this range, and the interpolated line ({{ F.class1_months_interp }} months) is illustrative, not a bound. The ceiling of {{ F.ceiling_12m }} at one year holds under both bounds.</p>
<div class="fig"><img src="{{ img.archetypes }}"></div>
<p class="cap">Figure 3. Calculator scores for six constructed profiles (published values, step-hold). Each profile is a documented case in <code>lab/run_lab.py</code>, not a sample of real people.</p>
<p><b>Reading the results.</b> The table rewards time in the file, and it does so by design: {{ v.vendor }} says points reflect the historical default rates of each group. A lower score for a short history is therefore not a defect in itself. It becomes a problem only if observed default rates for those groups do not support it, and that evidence is not public (F03). Two design features stand out. First, <i>entry dips</i>: holding no credit card earns 24 points, while a new card earns 0 to 23 in its first year, so opening a first product lowers the score for a while. Second, the established renter who moved six months ago (A2) still reaches Class 1 because her long card and contract history carries her; the address criterion mainly separates people who have stayed put for years from everyone else.</p>

<div class="fig"><img src="{{ img.sensitivity }}"></div>
<p class="cap">Figure 4. One event at a time, applied to the under-25 profile A3 (score {{ F.a3_score }}). Taking Mainkai's own 60-month loan costs {{ F.a3_loan60_drop }} points across three criteria (loans taken, remaining term, credit status) and drops the profile to Class 4 (F13).</p>

<!-- ============================== 3. LAB B ============================== -->
{% set B = bondora.s %}
<h2>3. Outcomes tests, run on Bondora's loan data (proxy evidence)</h2>
<p>Outcomes analysis needs outcomes, and none are public for {{ v.vendor }}'s score. Lab B therefore runs the tests Mainkai should require on its own retro-scored applicants (request E02) on a dataset where outcomes are public: Bondora's loan book. <b>Bondora is a different lender, with a different model, products, countries and default definition. Nothing below is evidence about {{ v.vendor }}.</b> The run shows what the tests find in a real rating, and why each one matters.</p>
<div class="box"><b>Data.</b> Bondora "Loan Dataset" investor export, downloaded by the author and supplied on 12 November 2026 (B03): {{ "{:,}".format(B.n_raw) }} loans issued from February 2009 to September 2026, SHA-256 <code>463277de6f9c</code>. <b>Exclusions:</b> {% for k, n in B.exclusions.items() %}{{ k }} {{ "{:,}".format(n) }}{% if not loop.last %}; {% endif %}{% endfor %}. <b>Analysed:</b> {{ "{:,}".format(B.n_analysed) }} loans. Score: Bondora's customer risk rating (AA best to G; HR has fewer than 30 loans and is suppressed). Target: Bondora's own flag for default within 12 months on book. Reference period {{ B.reference.window }} ({{ "{:,}".format(B.reference.n) }} loans); out-of-time {{ B.out_of_time.window }} ({{ "{:,}".format(B.out_of_time.n) }} loans; 2025 Q4 left out because only 23% of it is measurable yet). The reference starts in 2024 Q2 because loans from 2023 Q3 to 2024 Q1 belong to an earlier rating regime (quarterly Gini {{ F.early_gini_range }}, against {{ F.ref_gini }} for the reference). Using the full {{ F.ref_sens_window }} window instead gives a reference Gini of {{ F.ref_sens_gini }} and 2025 PSI of {{ F.ref_sens_psi }}, which overstates drift. Outcome availability was checked by issue month: every month to September 2025 is fully measurable, so the out-of-time sample is not selected on outcomes. Only aggregates of at least 30 loans leave the harness. The harness reads both Bondora formats through adapters and is tested on a synthetic fixture with planted defects (tests/test_outcomes_harness.py). Rerun: <code>make bondora CSV=data/bondora/loan_dataset_investor.parquet</code>.</div>
<table><thead><tr><th style="width:21%">Test</th><th>Result on Bondora's data</th><th style="width:15%">Mainkai tolerance</th><th style="width:13%">Reading</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Discrimination, out-of-time</td><td>Gini <b>{{ "%.3f"|format(B.out_of_time.gini) }}</b> (95% interval {{ "%.3f"|format(B.out_of_time.ci[0]) }} to {{ "%.3f"|format(B.out_of_time.ci[1]) }}) on 2025 loans; reference period {{ "%.3f"|format(B.reference.gini) }}. Interest rate alone: {{ "%.3f"|format(B.price_gini_oot) }}.</td><td>Reported for information; not a tolerance</td><td>Information only: an absolute Gini depends on the population scored</td></tr>
<tr><td>Champion-challenger, paired (method demonstration)</td><td>Rating against the interest rate as a stand-in for a current score, on the same out-of-time loans: Gini {{ F.cc_new }} against {{ F.cc_old }}, difference {{ F.cc_diff }} (95% bootstrap interval {{ F.cc_ci }}). The export holds one score, and Bondora sets its rates partly from the rating, so this shows the test, not a real comparison.</td><td>Lower bound of the difference above -{{ F.cc_loss }}</td><td><b>{{ "Passes" if F.cc_result == "no material loss" else "Fails" }}</b> (demonstration)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rank-ordering by grade</td><td>Out of time, {{ F.oot_break }}; in the reference period, {{ F.ref_break }}.</td><td>Rising in every band</td><td><b>Breaks at the tail</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Calibration of predicted PD</td><td>Not testable: the export gives grades but no PD per grade.</td><td>PD inside interval per band</td><td>Not testable</td></tr>
<tr><td>Grade-level drift</td><td>{{ F.grades_worse|capitalize }} grades defaulted more in 2025 than in the reference period ({{ F.drift_top }}), yet the overall rate was {{ F.dr_oot }} against {{ F.dr_ref }}, because the mix moved towards AA.</td><td>Stable per band</td><td><b>Drift hidden by mix</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Population stability (grade mix)</td><td>PSI against the reference period: {% for r in bondora.stab if r.quarter.startswith('2025') %}{{ r.quarter }} {{ "%.2f"|format(r.psi_vs_reference|float) }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}.</td><td>Below 0.10 green; 0.10 to 0.25 amber</td><td><b>{{ F.psi_2025_reading }}</b> in 2025 ({{ F.psi_2025_bands }})</td></tr>
<tr><td>Segment consistency (observed / expected given grade mix, same period)</td><td>{{ F.seg_extremes }}.</td><td>Close to 1</td><td><b>Inconsistent</b> across segments</td></tr>
<tr><td>Approval-rate ratio</td><td>Not testable: issued loans only.</td><td>At least 0.80</td><td>Not testable</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sensitivity: keep 14-day withdrawals</td><td>{% if bondora.sens %}Out-of-time Gini {{ "%.3f"|format(bondora.sens.gini_oot) }}: no material change.{% endif %}</td><td>n/a</td><td>Robust</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<div class="fig"><img style="max-width:150mm" src="{{ bondora.img_ro }}"></div>
<p class="cap">Figure 5. Bondora 12-month default rate by grade, reference period against the 2025 out-of-time sample, with 95% Wilson intervals. Proxy evidence about a different lender.</p>
<div class="fig"><img style="max-width:150mm" src="{{ bondora.img_seg }}"></div>
<p class="cap">Figure 6. Observed against expected 12-month defaults by segment, 2025 Q1 to Q3. Expected applies the period's grade default rates to each segment's own grade mix, so a ratio of 1 means a grade carries the same risk in that segment as overall. Income "not recorded" has fewer than 30 loans and is suppressed.</p>
<h3>What this means for the decision</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Compare scores on the same applicants, not levels.</b> An absolute Gini is not comparable across populations, and this sample holds approved loans only. The decisive test is paired: new against current score on the same retro-scored applicants, with a bootstrap interval for the difference (E02), for both the points score and any bands Mainkai will use.</li>
<li><b>A rising average is not enough.</b> Rank-ordering held across most of the scale but broke at the risky end, which is exactly where Mainkai's auto-decline cut-off will sit. Default rates per score band with intervals, per vintage, belong in E02.</li>
<li><b>The same score can mean different risk in different groups.</b> On this data observed defaults reached {{ F.oe_max }} times the expected level in one segment and {{ F.oe_min }} in another. Mainkai cannot see this for under-25s or recent arrivals without E04, and the thin-file ceiling in section 2 makes those the groups to test first.</li>
<li><b>Monitor by band, not just in aggregate.</b> A near-flat portfolio default rate hid worsening in {{ F.grades_worse }} grades. Mainkai's monitoring pack (E10) needs band-level observed-to-expected tracking.</li>
</ul>


<!-- ============================== 4. FINDINGS ============================== -->
<h2>4. Findings</h2>
<p>Each finding is labelled <i>fact</i> (sourced) or <i>assessment</i> (with its basis). Status describes the public record; the rating describes the consequence for Mainkai's decision.</p>
{% for f in findings %}
<div class="finding"><div class="hd"><b>{{ f.id }} · {{ s(f.title) }}</b><span><span class="tag">{{ f.basis }}</span> <span class="tag">{{ f.status.value }}</span> <span class="pill r-{{ f.rating.value }}">{{ f.rating.value }}</span></span></div>
