<!-- ============================== 5. CROSSWALK ============================== -->
<h2 class="pb">5. Model-risk crosswalk: elements A to N</h2>
<p>Elements follow SR 26-2 (which superseded SR 11-7 on 17 April 2026) and PRA SS1/23, anchored to EBA GL/2020/06 section 4.3.4 and MaRisk. <b>Core</b> marks the five elements that decide outcome rule 3.</p>
<div class="box"><b>Ratability test.</b> A core element can be rated only if some <i>result</i> is visible, not just the existence of a process. A reported figure, a published table or a stated review conclusion counts; a description of a procedure does not. The test is enforced in code (src/assurance/models.py): a core element cannot be marked ratable without a recorded visible result.</div>
<table class="small"><thead><tr><th style="width:5%">El.</th><th style="width:25%">Core element</th><th>Visible result on the public record</th><th style="width:9%">Rated?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for r in crosswalk if r.core %}<tr><td><b>{{ r.element }}</b></td><td>{{ r.name }}</td><td>{{ s(r.visible_result) if r.visible_result else '<b>None visible</b>' }}</td><td>{{ 'Yes' if r.ratable else '<b>No</b>' }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table>
<p><b>ECB guide to internal models:</b> not applied, as it covers IRB internal models, which are out of scope. Its machine-learning section (explainability, and performance that justifies complexity) is used only as a reference point for elements D and G.</p>
<table class="small"><thead><tr><th style="width:4%">El.</th><th style="width:13%">Element</th><th style="width:15%">Anchors</th><th style="width:26%">Public evidence</th><th style="width:20%">Lab result</th><th style="width:12%">Status</th><th style="width:7%">Rated?</th><th style="width:5%">F</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for r in crosswalk %}<tr><td><b>{{ r.element }}</b></td><td>{{ r.name }}{% if r.core %} <span class="tag">core</span>{% endif %}</td><td>{{ r.anchors }}</td><td>{{ s(r.public_evidence) }}</td><td>{{ s(r.lab) }}</td><td>{{ r.status.value }}</td><td>{{ 'Yes' if r.ratable else '<b>No</b>' }}</td><td>{{ r.findings|join(' ') }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table>

<!-- ============================== 6. AI ACT ============================== -->
<h2>6. AI Act: classification and readiness</h2>
<h3>Is it an AI system? A reasoned position</h3>
<p><b>The case for.</b> The points were learned from data by logistic regression, and {{ v.vendor }} describes the method as learning from examples and extracting statistical correlations (V12). The output is a prediction that shapes decisions about people, and Annex III point 5(b) names credit scoring of natural persons. <b>The case against.</b> Once fitted, the score is a fixed additive table. The Commission's guidelines on the definition place systems that improve or approximate well-established optimisation methods, naming linear and logistic regression, outside Article 3(1) (L09). Commentators dispute whether that carve-out reaches regression used to decide on people.</p>
<p><b>Position: treat the score as in scope, on a precautionary basis.</b> The downside of wrongly assuming it is out of scope falls on Mainkai, as deployer, and on applicants. Most of the controls are required anyway by GDPR, §37a BDSG, §505a BGB and MaRisk. This position would change if the Commission, BaFin or the CJEU said that fixed regression scorecards fall outside Article 3(1).</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Annex III point 5(b):</b> applies. The fraud-detection exception is not engaged.</li>
<li><b>Article 6(3) derogation:</b> unavailable. An Annex III system that profiles natural persons is always high-risk, and credit scoring is profiling under GDPR Article 4(4).</li>
<li><b>Timing and legacy rule:</b> Annex III obligations apply from 2 December 2027 (Regulation (EU) 2026/1744). The score was placed on the market on 17 March 2026, so under Article 111(2) it is caught only if its design changes significantly after that date (L08). A change made <i>before</i> 2 December 2027, for instance to criterion 4 for §37a, would not by itself bring the score into scope. A re-weighting after that date could. Mainkai should plan for the obligations and not rely on the exemption.</li>
<li><b>Supervisor:</b> since 29 July 2026, BaFin has been the AI Act market surveillance authority for creditworthiness systems of the institutions it supervises (KI-MIG, L10).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Deployer readiness (rows D1 to D10 of the obligation matrix)</h3>
<p>Ten provider rows are expected from {{ v.vendor }}. Five are not publicly available, four are available but inadequate, and one is not yet due. Mainkai's own rows are mostly within its control. Two depend on {{ v.vendor }}: D2 (use in line with instructions) and D7 (DPIA). Both are blocked until {{ v.vendor }} provides instructions for use (P5). The DPIA is needed now under GDPR Article 35, whatever the AI Act position. The full matrix is in Annex B.</p>
<div class="box"><b>FRIA outline (Article 27, one page in the repo).</b> <i>Process:</i> origination decline, pricing band and limit for the Ratenkredit. <i>Frequency:</i> continuous, about 120,000 applications a year. <i>Affected groups:</i> applicants aged 18 and over, with the thin-file groups named (under-25s, residents under 3 years, the self-employed). <i>Specific risks:</i> exclusion of short-history applicants, an address criterion under legal question, and data errors. <i>Oversight:</i> the refer band, override authority and the §37a(5) review route. <i>Remedies:</i> contest, data correction and complaints. <i>Notification:</i> to BaFin before first use. The FRIA needs E04 and E05 before it can be completed.</div>

<!-- ============================== 7. THIRD PARTY AND GERMAN LAW ============================== -->
<h2 class="pb">7. Third-party classification and German law</h2>
<h3>Which regime governs the relationship?</h3>
<p>The 9th MaRisk amendment (Circular 06/2026 (BA), 30 June 2026) removes ICT services under Article 3(21) DORA from AT 9 (L11). A relationship therefore falls under AT 9, under DORA third-party risk, or under neither. The plan's earlier assumption that the score was an "AT 9 outsourcing candidate" relied on the superseded 2023 circular.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th style="width:22%">Test</th><th>Analysis</th><th style="width:18%">Result</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>DORA ICT service (Art 3(21))</td><td>Score and report delivered on an ongoing basis through ICT systems (an API) as a data service.</td><td><b>Likely yes</b> (assessment)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Critical or important function (Art 3(22))</td><td>The score drives declines, pricing and limits for a core retail product. Its loss would stop automated origination.</td><td><b>Likely yes</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>MaRisk AT 9 outsourcing</td><td>Excluded if the relationship is a DORA ICT service. The credit decision stays with Mainkai in any case.</td><td>Not applicable</td></tr>
<tr><td>EBA/GL/2026/09 (non-ICT)</td><td>Only relevant if the service were found to be non-ICT. Final 18 Sep 2026; application date not yet confirmed.</td><td>Fallback only</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><b>Consequence.</b> Mainkai needs a register-of-information entry, the Article 30(2) and (3) contract provisions (service levels, audit and access rights, incident notification, exit support), an exit plan under Article 28(8), and concentration-risk analysis (F08). Model risk stays with Mainkai whatever the classification. SR 26-2 and EBA GL/2020/06 both hold the user responsible for understanding a vendor model.</p>
<h3>§37a BDSG from 20 November 2026</h3>
<p>Section 37a replaces §31 BDSG (BGBl. 2026 I Nr. 139). A probability value used to decide on a contract with a natural person may be created or used only if the following inputs are left out: special categories of data, age, gender, name, social-network data, account movements and <b>address data</b>. It also must not concern minors and must rest on a scientifically recognised method (L01, L04). On request, the creator must explain the data, the weighting of the most influential criteria and the meaning of the value (§37a(4)). The applicant can contest the decision and obtain a decision by a natural person from the lender (§37a(5)).</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Address data (F01).</b> Under the old §31, address data were allowed as long as they were not used exclusively. The new rule removes that allowance. Whether <i>age of current address</i> counts as address data is untested. The criterion measures duration rather than location, but it is derived from address records, and {{ v.vendor }} has said nothing public about it. This is the most time-critical item in the review.</li>
<li><b>Age.</b> The score does not use the applicant's age. Time-based criteria correlate with age, but §37a bans age as an input, not correlated inputs. This is a fairness and bias question (F03), not a §37a breach.</li>
<li><b>Minors.</b> The scope population is 18 and over, so this condition is met.</li>
</ul>
<h3>GDPR Articles 15 and 22, and the courts</h3>
<p>Under {{ v.case_cjeu }}, a bureau's score can itself be an automated decision when a lender draws strongly on it. In {{ v.case_bgh }}, the BGH set aside four appellate judgments against {{ v.vendor }} and remanded them. It held that the Article 15(1)(h) right to information about the logic exists only where all elements of Article 22(1) are met. It eased the burden of proof: if a score was transmitted and an adverse decision followed in time, decisiveness is presumed (L05). <b>{{ V }} won those appeals; the rulings did not create a general new right.</b> The broader right comes from §37a(4) from 20 November 2026. CJEU C-203/22 sets the content of an explanation: the procedure and principles actually applied, in a form the person can understand and use to contest (L07). For Mainkai, auto-declines below the cut-off will normally meet the presumption. Mainkai is also itself making an Article 22 decision, which needs a lawful basis under Article 22(2) and Article 22(3) safeguards.</p>
<h3>§§505a ff. BGB (CCD2) from 20 November 2026</h3>
<p>The creditworthiness test now requires that repayment be <i>likely</i>. Where the assessment uses automated processing, the consumer can request human intervention, an explanation and a review. §505b(2) and (3) BGB restrict data sources, excluding special categories and social-network data. The score is one input to Mainkai's test, not the test itself. Affordability remains out of scope.</p>

