<h2>12. Limitations</h2>
<ul>
<li>Public sources only. Sources were captured as text excerpts, and the hashes cover those excerpts. A byte-exact archive (Playwright, original PDFs) is an open QA item.</li>
<li>Criteria 5, 8 and 9, the first-year address value and the score-class boundaries were first taken from two agreeing secondary sources and then confirmed by the author on {{ v.vendor }}'s own pages on 13 November 2026 (class boundaries V21; criterion values V22). The bureau's text for criterion 9 overlaps at exactly three years ('up to three years' 61, 'three years or more' 48); because it also says terms of up to three years have no negative impact, the calculator scores exactly 36 months as 61. The full band table is still requested (E06).</li>
<li>The calculator describes the published table. It cannot reproduce rounding, grace rules, debt-collection effects or data mapping.</li>
<li>Bondora results describe a different lender, model, population and default definition. The export has no PD, model version or age, so calibration, version comparison and age segments could not be tested on it.</li>
<li>The Bondora sample holds approved loans only. Applicants Bondora rejected are missing, so the riskiest part of the population is absent; this compresses the score range and lowers Gini whatever the score's design. The proxy's Gini level ({{ F.oot_gini }}) is therefore not comparable with the bureau's reported 0.6, which is one reason Mainkai's tolerance is a paired comparison, and approval rates and reject inference cannot be tested on it.</li>
<li>The legal analysis is a reading by a non-lawyer, and the §37a address question is genuinely open.</li>
<li>Prepared without practitioner review. The findings, ratings, core-element choices and tolerances are the author's own judgement and have not been challenged by a second reviewer; the scenario test in section 8 shows how the outcome moves when the evidence changes.</li>
</ul>

